  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Over the past 3 years, Business Association JIA developed its informal economy policy portfolio and established itself as the leading business organization for influencing formalization of enterprises policies. JIA invested in improving its statistical and quantitative capacity to conduct research on the institutional and legislative framework related to formalization. In November 2019, JIA presented two research reports on the informal economy to audience made of 50 government representatives, national institutions, international organizations, and media. JIA&apos;s reports created great interest among all participants, with specific appreciation expressed by representatives of labour inspection, and led to an intensive dialogue. The reports also attracted interest from international institutions (European Union, Foreign embassies, International Monetary Fund) as well as JIA members who appreciated the effort to tackle this important issue. Following these requests, JIA developed a policy paper on the informal economy based on the outcomes and findings of the two research reports. In September 2021, JIA launched the policy paper, and promoted it.
To supplement its on-going work on the informal economy during the COVID crisis, JIA launched an additional piece of research on the effects of COVID on the economy with a focus on the informal economy.  In September 2021, JIA presented the report to stakeholders, which further increased JIA`s visibility and advocacy standing among enterprises, government and stakeholders. In April 2018, with the contribution of RBSA, and in cooperation with JIA, the National Alliance of Business Associations and the Confederation of Employers, the ILO organized 2 focus groups discussions (FGD) with 10 employers each and a seminar for employers to discuss the challenges of the informal economy. The inputs of these FGD and of the seminar discussion were used by ILO to develop a questionnaire to be used in a research among the enterprise sector.
In May 2019, the ILO supported JIA for the research among enterprises on the informal economy and the challenges of formalization. The ILO provided JIA with the know-how and expert support in deployment of the survey among the representative sample of 500 companies. ILO supported the analysis of the survey data and development of the research report.
In July 2019, the ILO provided JIA with support in researching institutional and legislative framework for formalization, and in development of the report on the findings of the research. 
In November 2019, the ILO assisted JIA in the organization of a round table at which the two research reports were presented to representatives of the Government, national and international institutions, media and public.
With the contribution of RBSA, the ILO provided support to JIA in developing a policy paper on the informal economy, including a set of proposals aimed at facilitation of transition to formality (February-August 2021). In June-September 2021, the ILO supported the JIA to develop a report on the impact of COVID-19 on enterprises, including informal enterprises, by providing a framework of survey questionnaires and supporting the JIA to tailor the questionnaire to the local reality of Kirghizstan. In July-August 2021, the ILO supported JIA with expertise in the adaptation and promotion of relevant informal economy policy briefs.</narrative>
   </description>
